I want to do kind of a head and heart  check of this moment of time wherein how  are you doing how are you feeling about  the state of the world and the movement  that's happening outside our doors as  you know I've always been very vocal  about justice for african-american men  in particular it's just really always at  the forefront of talking about race  relations and so this is nothing new to  me  but it's very exciting to see the whole  world participating in this whole  movement because it's just not the  United States you know I see them  protesting in Paris London the Middle  East and so that's refreshing I'm  hopeful that positive things will come  from this what I think is very different  about this moment in time is because we  have such a divisive leader that it  basically has propelled this movement  because when you've got a leader who's  leading like our president is leading it  allows for this type of energy and so  wow you know some might see it as a very  bad thing like I'm telling people you  know just like we saw you know the whole  movement for women you know sexual  harassment you know I think this is the  about time movement it's about time we  have this very hard discussion because  there's a very sorted pass with race  relations throughout the world not just  the United States but throughout the  world but in particularly in this  country yesterday actually directed race  art  Brooks's funeral so that was a very  heavy moment but I was you know happy to  serve the family happy to have moments  with this you know it's beautiful wife  their beautiful children and you know  just to be present  [Music]  following the funeral his wife Tamika  Miller she requested that we take her by  the Wendy's where the incident occurred  and I rode with her in the car and we  had such a very point conversation about  this moment in time because she said to  me I never knew that I would be in this  place you know and I turn turn I said  but this place is a place of purpose a  place of power because you're now the  face of a entirely new movement within  the movement and so you know she said he  had always talked about being you know  well known and doing things that would  change the world and I told I said you  know sometimes we don't reach true  fruition until you know unfortunately  until we have left the world and I mean  we see that so many people in death  there is greatness sometimes and so you  know no one wants to die but it in depth  there sometimes becomes the start of a  movement and so you know George Floyd  started the movement rashard added  another effort to the flame  to make it even bigger and you know we  grow through that Wendy's it was  unbelievable how many people were out  there still with their hands up you know  saying you know black lives matter with  you you know we took the casket and you  know the flower cart and the entire  family around the drive-through you know  the same place where you know the fatal  shots were fired and so to be there with  his beautiful you know these young  children our one-year-old daughter  two-year-old daughter eight-year-old  daughter his son mother law in the same  car into you know just feel the power  the energy of them being able to release  the hurt that they had from that tragic  incident it was  moving to me as a person of color but as  a mother you know it just gave me a  whole new sense of purpose because we  have to propel this movement we have to  have this conversation we have to change  policy procedures laws we have to make  sure that there is equality for everyone  and so it was it was a beautiful  ceremony so many people came out I've  never seen a funeral ceremony and I've  been in this business now for about 13  years you know and so I've never seen a  ceremony a funeral home going as we call  in our culture where there were there  were more black men at this funeral than  I've ever seen in my life I mean it it  was unbelievable how much support from  the community and you know just  celebrities that came out and so to see  everyone come together unified in our  city which is a you know this is the  movement the mecca of the civil rights  movement it was it was beautiful and so  I'm just happy that I was able to serve  the family and to be of service you know  at that moment you are raising two black  sons what kind of conversations are you  having with them in this moment and this  is what's difficult I think because it  was such a harsh experience for our  grandparents and our parents from my  generation I think they tried to shield  us with it because they didn't want us  to know the pain that came with telling  the true story of what it felt like to  be discriminate against my mother  integrated her I think her middle school  or high school and so you know she  speaks about it but growing up it wasn't  as if I knew all the horrors of racism  because you know we lived in the  middle-class community my parents  weren't you know schoolteachers pastors  and so you know I grew up you know in a  very nice environment I've always you  know lived amongst people who are very  diverse  you know growing up I was always taught  you know when you go out the store you  get a receipt in the bag because you  don't want anyone's accused you of  stealing you know don't put your hands  in your pockets because you don't want  anyone to think you're doing something  that could lead you know you getting in  trouble I was always taught to say you  know yes sir no sir to anybody in a  position of authority but I think as  we've elevated to levels you know of  wealth you know and you start to really  become very integrated into a different  socioeconomic status you really stop  telling those very difficult stories to  your children and so now you know my  children obviously saw the George Floyd  incident on television could not  understand why that was happening and so  my youngest son was very much so and you  know why why did 